arXiv:2510.04019v1 [cs.LG] 05 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Anthony Zhan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Stanford University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">azhan9@stanford.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion large language models (dLLMs) are a new paradigm of non-autoregressive language models that are trained to predict multiple tokens in parallel and generate text via iterative unmasking. Recent works have successfully pretrained dLLMs to parity with autoregressive LLMs at the 8B scale, but dLLMs have yet to benefit from modern post-training techniques, e.g. reinforcement learning (RL), that have proven effective for autoregressive models. Crucially, algorithms designed for traditional LLMs aren’t directly compatible with diffusion frameworks due to inherent differences in modeling assumptions. Moreover, existing attempts at dLLM post-training with RL rely on heuristic-based objectives with no theoretical grounding. In this work, we present Amortized Group Relative Policy Optimization (AGRPO), a principled on-policy RL algorithm designed specifically for dLLMs. AGRPO uses Monte Carlo sampling to compute an unbiased policy gradient estimate, making it the first tractable, faithful adaptation of policy gradient methods for dLLMs. We demonstrate AGRPO’s effectiveness on different math/reasoning tasks, a common setting for RL with LLMs, achieving up to +7.6% absolute gain on GSM8K and 3.8x performance on the Countdown task over the baseline LLaDA-8B-Instruct model and 1.3x performance gains over comparable RL methods such as diffu-GRPO. Furthermore, these gains persist across different numbers of sampling steps at inference time, achieving better tradeoffs between compute and performance. Our results demonstrate that online RL algorithms can be extended to diffusion LLMs in principled ways, maintaining both theoretical soundness and practical effectiveness.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many recent efforts in LLM research have centered around reinforcement learning, specifically in the verifiable reward (RLVR) setting. In a typical setup, base models are trained on math or coding problems and incentivized to reason through the solution step-by-step, getting a reward if the final answer is correct. The main goal of RLVR is to elicit mathematical thinking/reasoning capabilities, allowing models to solve complex real-world tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This wave of interest in RL and reasoning, initially spurred by models like OpenAI’s o1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and DeepSeek’s R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, has led to the development of numerous RL algorithms designed specifically for transformer-based autoregressive (AR) LLMs. With the success of these algorithms, among them Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, AR LLMs have grown incredibly strong on problem-solving benchmarks, often matching or exceeding human expert level. Closed frontier models have even achieved gold medal performance at international math and coding competitions, a remarkable feat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luong &amp; Lockhart, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin &amp; Cheng, 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In a parallel line of research, dLLMs have recently emerged as a more versatile alternative to the traditional autoregressive paradigm. Continuous diffusion models have long been established as the dominant framework for image and video generation, relying on a denoising/score matching objective. Works such as D3PM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and SEDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> successfully transferred this diffusion framework to discrete settings, including language. Successive efforts such as MDLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and RADD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2025</a>)</cite> have simplified and merged different formulations, with consensus settling on a masked token prediction objective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While autoregressive models are trained to predict the next token in left-to-right order, dLLMs are trained to predict an <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">arbitrary</em> number of tokens in <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">arbitrary</em> positions. Current state-of-the-art dLLMs, such as LLaDA-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and MMaDA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, are usually almost on par with open-source AR models such as LLaMA3-8B and Qwen2.5-7B on common NLP benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Once trained, these models can generate text with greater flexibility than standard left-to-right prompting (e.g. infilling) by starting with a completely or partially masked sequence and iteratively unmasking tokens. In addition, dLLMs have the benefit of being able to trade off compute and quality at inference time: one can decrease the number of steps/forward passes to save compute at the cost of slightly worse generations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. However, these models still struggle to match AR models in downstream tasks that require long-form thinking and reasoning. This discrepancy stems from fundamental challenges in applying existing post-training techniques to dLLMs: autoregressive models can easily compute exact probabilities for all tokens with a single forward pass, whereas diffusion models only compute probabilities one denoising step at a time, making them unamenable to current RL formulations. Unlocking true reasoning capabilities would be a giant leap forward for dLLMs, solidifying them as a true rival of AR LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our work helps dLLMs close this gap by proposing a principled, unbiased form of GRPO designed especially for dLLMs: Amortized GRPO (AGRPO). Through a simple modification of the original GPRO objective — by viewing the inner sum over all tokens as an expectation over timesteps — we make dLLMs compatible with the well-established realm of online RL with LLMs and pave the way for future dLLM-inspired works.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04019v1/algs.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:543/199;" width="543" height="199"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>A comparison of different RL post-training algorithms for dLLMs. Existing algorithms designed for traditional LLMs such as GRPO require token-level probabilities, which would entail <math id="S1.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\text{response length})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>response length</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\text{response length})</annotation></semantics></math> forward passes. Current tractable techniques for dLLMs involve heuristic approximations, resulting in biased policy gradients. Our proposed algorithm takes a different approach that remains tractable and faithful to the original GRPO objective.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Soundness.</span> We derived a modified form of GRPO’s objective (which is itself derived from well-known policy gradient methods) that is both tractable for dLLMs and theoretically sound thanks to its unbiased nature. We also explain why comparable methods, based on heuristic approximations of token probabilities, fail to be theoretically sound.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency.</span> We explain how to practically implement our proposed algorithm in a way that is both memory- and compute-efficient, and discuss various tradeoffs surrounding implementation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficacy.</span> We train models using AGRPO on three mathematical reasoning tasks: GSM8K, MATH, and Countdown, achieving (absolute) accuracy gains of 7.6%, 5.2%, and 29.6%, respectively. In addition, we show that AGRPO consistently retains high accuracy when evaluated with different numbers of sampling steps, completely redefining the inference compute/quality frontier.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Understanding diffusion language models</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Pretraining</h3>

<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04019v1/pretraining.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:411/98;" width="411" height="98"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Models under the autoregressive and diffusion paradigms are trained on different objectives. Next-token prediction (left) is a narrower, easily parallelizable task, whereas masked token prediction (right) is harder and less conducive to parallelism since it involves predicting multiple tokens. Diffusion models must also optimize for a lower bound rather than the exact likelihood.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most common form of discrete diffusion for language is the masked (or “absorbing”) approach, where models are trained to reverse data corrupted by randomly masking tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arriola et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Concretely, given a distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> on sequences of discrete tokens <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, models are trained to maximize the following evidence-based lower bound (ELBO) on the likelihood <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}t\sim U[0,1]\\
x,x^{t}\sim p^{t}\end{subarray}}\left[\frac{1}{t}\sum_{x^{t}_{i}=\blacksquare}\log p_{\theta}(x_{i}\mid x^{t})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}t\sim U[0,1]\\
x,x^{t}\sim p^{t}\end{subarray}}\left[\frac{1}{t}\sum_{x^{t}_{i}=\blacksquare}\log p_{\theta}(x_{i}\mid x^{t})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,x^{t}\sim p^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,x^{t}\sim p^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> means that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by independently setting each token <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the mask token <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math> with probability <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            One can derive an equivalent discrete ELBO that samples over <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with exactly <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>’s, summing from <math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="footnote1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, which is empirically more stable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</span></span></span> This masking process is also known as the “forward process.” For example, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> might have 3 <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>’s after the forward process, so the objective would be a sum of 3 cross-entropy terms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One can view equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Pretraining ‣ 2 Understanding diffusion language models ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> as a weighted sum of BERT-like cross-entropy objectives, where the models learn to “denoise” data at varying levels of noise, from clean data to pure noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2025)</a></cite>. In other words, the model learns to predict marginal distributions of tokens given arbitrary context. Note that transformer-based diffusion models typically use full self-attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peebles &amp; Xie, 2022</a>)</cite>, allowing them to use context from both the left and right.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The crucial difference compared to the classic AR cross-entropy objective is that this is a harder/more general task (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Pretraining ‣ 2 Understanding diffusion language models ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>): in each training example, many tokens in different positions can be masked, and the model must learn to perform a single denoising step over all masked tokens. (This is a strict <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">superset</em> of the next-token prediction task.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, AR models constrain themselves to only consider the single next token in left-to-right order. The benefits of imposing this causal constraint are twofold: it lets training be parallelized so that transformer models can learn to generate the whole example with a single forward pass, and it also lets AR models maximize the exact likelihood (via the chain rule) rather than a lower bound.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Inference</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite a more complicated training setup, dLLMs enjoy several benefits at inference time: they can generate text in arbitrary order, are naturally self-speculative (i.e. one can see the model’s best guess for the entire sequence at every step), and can trade off compute and generation quality by choosing to unmask more or less tokens per step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To generate text, dLLMs start with an all- or partially-masked sequence, obtain marginal distributions for each masked token, and then unmask some of these by sampling from their marginals. The tokens to be unmasked can be chosen either randomly, adhering to the theoretical “backward pass,” or by keeping the tokens with highest probability, as proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al. (2025)</a></cite>. (We refer to these as “random” and “low-confidence” unmasking, respectively.) The rest of the tokens are kept the same, and this new sequence is fed back into the model; this process is repeated until all tokens are unmasked.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this paper, we use <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> to refer to the number of sampling steps/forward passes, and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to refer to the sequence length. Typically, the number of tokens unmasked at each step <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/m</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen to be relatively small (<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>) — unmasking higher tokens at each step severely degrades quality as measured by perplexity/accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. (Note that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=m</annotation></semantics></math> for autoregressive models.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremarkx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremarkx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmremarkx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremarkx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremarkx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One drawback of dLLMs is that the context length must be fixed ahead of time, instead of being dynamically grown as with AR LLMs. Works such as BlockDiff address this issue by introducing a hybrid autoregressive/diffusion framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arriola et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; we stay within the normal diffusion framework for simplicity.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>From PPO to AGRPO</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>The RL setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By viewing tokens as the action space, context as the state, and language models as neural network-parameterized policies <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can cast the problem of post-training LLMs as an online reinforcement learning (RL) problem. Diffusion models naturally fit into this RL framework thanks to their multi-step denoising nature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025a)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Early attempts at RL with LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> borrowed standard policy gradient methods such as Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), showing that PPO could improve reasoning abilities beyond SFT. Notably, models trained with RL picked up emergent capabilities such as backtracking and self-correction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A necessary step in PPO is computing the reward <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and value <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for each step <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, which are combined with Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE) to produce advantage estimates <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. However, as LLMs have grown in size, the intermediate step of training separate reward and value models has become extremely cumbersome.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In RLVR environments, including many math and coding datasets, one can easily obtain ground truth rewards without an external model, which gets rid of the reward model. Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), a widely used algorithm proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. (2024)</a></cite>, gets rid of the value model by computing advantages using the mean reward across a group of rollouts, further reducing memory and compute costs. Base models trained with GRPO showed much stronger performance across math benchmarks and even exhibited “A-ha moments” indicative of genuine mathematical reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>The GRPO objective</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To introduce notation, let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution over questions <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o^{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o^{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> a group of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> outputs (or rollouts) generated from policy <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{old}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{old}</annotation></semantics></math> (which can optionally be offline), <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the respective rewards, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=\frac{r_{i}-\operatorname{mean}\{r_{i}\}}{\operatorname{std}\{r_{i}\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>std</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=\frac{r_{i}-\operatorname{mean}\{r_{i}\}}{\operatorname{std}\{r_{i}\}}</annotation></semantics></math> the (normalized) advantage estimates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that rewards in the RLVR setting are outcome-based, so we receive a single reward per rollout. This means the advantage estimate <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> applies equally to all tokens in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">GRPO’s objective function,</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}q\sim D\\
\{o^{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{old}(\cdot\mid q)\end{subarray}}\left[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|o^{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|o^{i}|}\operatorname{clip}\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}{\pi_{old}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})},1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\right)A_{i}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>o</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}q\sim D\\
\{o^{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{old}(\cdot\mid q)\end{subarray}}\left[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|o^{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|o^{i}|}\operatorname{clip}\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}{\pi_{old}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})},1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\right)A_{i}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">naturally lends itself to AR LLMs, where the action probabilities for all steps can be computed in a single forward pass. (For clarity, the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>min</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> terms have been omitted from the inner sum.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, trying to apply this algorithm to dLLMs out-of-the-box would be infeasible, as dLLMs would require a separate forward pass for <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">each</em> term in the inner sum. This discrepancy between AR and diffusion models is the same as the discrepancy in pretraining discussed in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Pretraining ‣ 2 Understanding diffusion language models ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> — autoregressive models can obtain exact token probabilities for every step in parallel, while dLLMs must obtain them sequentially. A naive implementation would require <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(|o^{i}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(|o^{i}|)</annotation></semantics></math> forward passes per gradient step, which is simply intractable for online RL.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Previous attempts at dLLM post-training</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although dLLM post-training progress remains relatively quiet, there have been several attempts to adapt AR post-training techniques such as GRPO. Here we summarize two recent proposals for dLLM-specific RL algorithms and explain why they fail to hold up under theoretical analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">diffu-GRPO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2025)</a></cite> propose a two-stage dLLM post-training process consisting of SFT on reasoning traces followed by RLVR. We focus on their proposed RL algorithm, diffu-GRPO. Instead of spending <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> forward passes to compute the GRPO objective <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 The GRPO objective ‣ 3 From PPO to AGRPO ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, diffu-GRPO estimates all token probabilities under a <em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single</em> denoising step, i.e. they fully mask the output and approximate <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. They also introduce random masking to the prompt (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in a manner similar to dLLM pretraining, which they claim helps stabilize training. However, there is an evident mismatch with respect to RL assumptions: the rollouts are not generated in a single step, and the context/state clearly matters when the model is unmasking tokens (i.e. choosing actions).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UniGRPO</span> MMaDA is a multimodal dLLM developed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a></cite> which is post-trained by UniGRPO, an algorithm that unifies RL training across different tasks and modalities. Identifying the lack of output masking in diffu-GRPO as a potential flaw, they propose randomly masking the entire sequence multiple times and approximating token probabilities by averaging across samples where that token is masked. More formally, given rollout <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a randomly noised version of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> where each token is independently masked with probability <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (as in dLLM pretraining). UniGRPO assumes</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(o_{t}\mid q,o_{&lt;t})\approx\mathbb{E}_{\alpha\sim U[0,1]}\left[\bm{1}_{\{o^{\alpha}_{t}=\blacksquare\}}\pi_{\theta}(o^{\alpha}_{t}\mid q,o^{\alpha})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>o</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(o_{t}\mid q,o_{&lt;t})\approx\mathbb{E}_{\alpha\sim U[0,1]}\left[\bm{1}_{\{o^{\alpha}_{t}=\blacksquare\}}\pi_{\theta}(o^{\alpha}_{t}\mid q,o^{\alpha})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and approximates the RHS by sampling <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            To ensure every token is masked at least once, the algorithm enforces <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> for the last sample. This breaks theoretical soundness since samples are no longer distributed <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim U[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim U[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> This follows the heuristic from pretraining where the model learns to recover the true output from randomly added noise and context.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremarkx2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremarkx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmremarkx2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremarkx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremarkx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The GRPO objective also includes a KL penalty <math id="Thmremarkx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{ref})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{ref})</annotation></semantics></math> term which diffu-GRPO and MMaDA both interpret as a sequence-level KL divergence. Since diffusion models don’t allow for exact likelihood calculation, both works further assume <math id="Thmremarkx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}\mid q)\approx\sum_{t=1}^{m}\pi_{\theta}(o_{t}\mid q,o_{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}\mid q)\approx\sum_{t=1}^{m}\pi_{\theta}(o_{t}\mid q,o_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math>, which doesn’t strictly hold under the diffusion framework.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Although both methods proposed above obtained positive empirical results, they both suffer from a reliance on biased, one-step approximations of token probabilities. This approach makes RL tractable for dLLMs, but comes at the cost of inconsistency with the underlying RL framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The root cause of this inconsistency is that unlike dLLM pretraining, RL policies must take into account their multi-step nature and assign action probabilities in a context-dependent way. With this consideration in mind, we propose a new algorithm called Amortized Group Relative Policy Optimization (AGRPO). AGRPO is the first <em id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">principled</em> adaptation of policy gradient methods to dLLMs that computes an <em id="S3.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unbiased</em> policy gradient estimate by dropping unnecessary approximations in favor of exact token probabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Amortized GRPO</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Deriving the AGRPO objective</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show how to reinterpret the GRPO objective as an expectation across timesteps with respect to the uniform measure. Consider the inner sum in equation <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 The GRPO objective ‣ 3 From PPO to AGRPO ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, with clipping removed for clarity:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{m}\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}{\pi_{old}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}A_{i}=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\{1,\dots,m\}}\left[\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}{\pi_{old}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}A_{i}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{m}\sum_{t=1}^{m}\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}{\pi_{old}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}A_{i}=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\{1,\dots,m\}}\left[\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}{\pi_{old}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}A_{i}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|o^{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|o^{i}|</annotation></semantics></math> has been replaced by <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of sampling steps (which may be <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;|o^{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;|o^{i}|</annotation></semantics></math> if multiple tokens are unmasked per step).<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025b)</a></cite> show that even with AR models, replacing the <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{|o^{i}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{|o^{i}|}</annotation></semantics></math> term by a constant <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{C}</annotation></semantics></math> helps mitigate response length bias.</span></span></span> The RHS can now be estimated via Monte Carlo (MC) sampling by drawing <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\ll m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\ll m</annotation></semantics></math> timesteps from the uniform distribution on <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>, computing the <em id="S4.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exact</em> inner terms, and averaging (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following subsequent improvements to GRPO, such as Dr. GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, we also use unnormalized advantages <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=r_{i}-\operatorname{mean}\{r_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=r_{i}-\operatorname{mean}\{r_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This helps avoid bias from particularly easy or hard problems where advantages have low variance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The full AGRPO objective is</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}q\sim D\\
\{o^{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{old}(\cdot\mid q)\end{subarray}}\\
\left[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\{1,\dots,m\}}\left[\min\left(\rho^{i}_{t}A_{i},\operatorname{clip}\left(\rho^{i}_{t},1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\right)A_{i}\right)-\beta D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\,||\,\pi_{ref})\right]\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>o</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>min</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>clip</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.507em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}q\sim D\\
\{o^{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{old}(\cdot\mid q)\end{subarray}}\\
\left[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\{1,\dots,m\}}\left[\min\left(\rho^{i}_{t}A_{i},\operatorname{clip}\left(\rho^{i}_{t},1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\right)A_{i}\right)-\beta D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\,||\,\pi_{ref})\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{i}_{t}=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}{\pi_{old}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{i}_{t}=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}{\pi_{old}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">and the KL term represents the divergence at timestep <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})\,||\,\pi_{ref}(\cdot\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})\,||\,\pi_{ref}(\cdot\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t}))</annotation></semantics></math>. By keeping the KL term inside the expectation, we can approximate it via MC sampling as well. We use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman (2020)</a></cite>’s unbiased KL estimator, which is also used by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.1 Deriving the AGRPO objective ‣ 4 Amortized GRPO ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides an overview of our proposed algorithm.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Amortized Group Relative Policy Optimization (AGRPO)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
              
              
             policy <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, # sampling steps <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, # MC samples <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref}\leftarrow\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}\leftarrow\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> not converged <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{old}\leftarrow\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{old}\leftarrow\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              sample prompt <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              sample rollouts <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{o^{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{old}(\cdot\mid q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o^{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{old}(\cdot\mid q)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              compute advantages <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
                select <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\{1,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\{1,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly with low-discrepancy sampler (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Low-discrepancy sampling ‣ 4 Amortized GRPO ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
                <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{i}_{t}\leftarrow\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}{\pi_{old}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{i}_{t}\leftarrow\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}{\pi_{old}(o^{i}_{t}\mid q,o^{i}_{&lt;t})}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
                <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{j}\leftarrow\sum_{i=1}^{G}\left[\min\left(\rho^{i}_{t}A_{i},\operatorname{clip}\left(\rho^{i}_{t},1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\right)A_{i}\right)-\beta D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\,||\,\pi_{ref})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">←</mo><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>clip</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{j}\leftarrow\sum_{i=1}^{G}\left[\min\left(\rho^{i}_{t}A_{i},\operatorname{clip}\left(\rho^{i}_{t},1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\right)A_{i}\right)-\beta D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\,||\,\pi_{ref})\right]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               compute AGRPO objective <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\frac{1}{kG}\sum_{j=1}^{k}M_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\frac{1}{kG}\sum_{j=1}^{k}M_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               backpropagate loss and take gradient step w.r.t. <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="Thmremarkx3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremarkx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmremarkx3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremarkx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremarkx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although our algorithm includes <math id="Thmremarkx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> as a hyperparameter, for this paper we adopt the common practice of assuming <math id="Thmremarkx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so the algorithm is fully on-policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025a)</a></cite>. Other hyperparameters, including <math id="Thmremarkx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G,\varepsilon," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G,\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremarkx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, can be found in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1.SSS1" title="A.1.1 Hyperparameters ‣ A.1 Experiment details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Low-discrepancy sampling</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, a naive MC sampling algorithm would draw <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples and average them. However, we can actually reduce the variance further by introducing correlation across samples so that they collectively “cover” a wide range of timesteps while ensuring that the marginal distribution for each sample is still uniform on <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is known as low-discrepancy sampling, and is used in practice to lower training variance for both continuous and discrete diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2025)</a></cite>’s discrete low-discrepancy sampler, which works as follows:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sample <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. from <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U([0,1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U([0,1])</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">“Bin” them into <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint bins by defining <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j}^{\prime}=(u_{j}+j)/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}^{\prime}=(u_{j}+j)/k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define the final samples <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{j}\in\{1,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{j}\in\{1,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{j}=\lfloor mu_{j}^{\prime}\rfloor+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{j}=\lfloor mu_{j}^{\prime}\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Low-discrepancy sampling induces the desirable property that as <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> approaches <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, we fully recreate the original GRPO objective. In other words, one can achieve higher fidelity by scaling the amount of compute.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Practical considerations</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we discuss various decisions and tradeoffs made regarding the actual implementation of AGRPO. As with all online RL algorithms, the goal of any implementation is to run as efficiently as possible while maximizing efficacy. A more detailed discussion follows in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Further practical considerations ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1 </span>Caching partially masked states</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to obtain exact token probabilities, we must recreate the exact state/context at the step where that token was unmasked. To do this efficiently, we cache the unmasking order during generation so that each token is associated with a timestep <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, to get the partially masked state at timestep <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we simply mask out all tokens with timestep <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2 </span>Choosing <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When choosing the number of Monte Carlo samples <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to use, the immediate tradeoff is clear: increasing <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> lowers variance but costs more compute. However, another important piece to consider here is the generation time: in online RL, the cost of generating rollouts often dwarfs the cost of computing the loss (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m)</annotation></semantics></math> forward passes vs. <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> forward and backward pass per iteration). dLLMs especially suffer from long inference times due to full self-attention and the lack of KV caching. Thus we can choose moderately large <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> without significantly increasing wall time, as long as <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\ll m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\ll m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that one can accumulate the gradient immediately after each MC sample is computed by calling <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">loss.backward()</span> (without taking an optimizer step) <em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inside</em> the for loop. This frees the computational graph and avoids excess memory usage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, we do indeed observe more stable training and faster convergence for larger values of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the matter of choosing <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is mostly up to one’s discretion; see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1.SSS1" title="A.1.1 Hyperparameters ‣ A.1 Experiment details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1.1</span></a> for specific values of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> chosen in our experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To empirically validate our proposed algorithm, we start from the state-of-the-art open source dLLM LLaDA-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and train three separate models using AGRPO on three different reasoning tasks: GSM8K, MATH, and Countdown. GSM8K and MATH are standard problem-solving benchmarks consisting of 8.5k and 12.5k math problems at the grade school and high school level, respectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Countdown is a popular math reasoning task where the model is given a list of 3-4 numbers and a target number; the goal is to combine the numbers using arithmetic operations (<math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+,-,\times,/" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">×</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">/</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+,-,\times,/</annotation></semantics></math>) and parentheses to get the target number <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Due to compute constraints, we use Low-Rank Adaptation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and fix the response length to be <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=384</annotation></semantics></math> for GSM8K + MATH and <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=256</annotation></semantics></math> for Countdown. We set <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>192</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=192</annotation></semantics></math> for GSM8K and <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=128</annotation></semantics></math> for MATH + Countdown, so that 2-3 tokens are unmasked per step. This balances inference wall time and stability, both of which are critical for online RL.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">During training, we generate rollouts with random remasking to inject more stochasticity into the environment and incentivize exploration. We switch to low confidence remasking (which can be thought of as a form of annealing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite>) for evaluation. Accuracies are reported with <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=512,m=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=512,m=256</annotation></semantics></math> for MATH and <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=256,m=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=256,m=128</annotation></semantics></math> for GSM8K + Countdown.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Results</h3>

<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Reported accuracies for different reasoning tasks across different RL post-training methods. (baseline) refers to DeepSeekMath-Base 7B for autoregressive and LLaDA-8B-Instruct for diffusion. All tasks are 0-shot pass@1; the best accuracy for each model type and task is <span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bolded</span>.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;" colspan="2"></th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;" colspan="3">Accuracy (%)</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Model type</th>
<th id="S5.T1.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Method</th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">GSM8K</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">MATH</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Countdown</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;" rowspan="3"><span id="S5.T1.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Autoregressive</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">(baseline)</th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">64.2</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">36.2</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">-</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">CoT SFT</th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">82.9</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">46.8</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">-</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">GRPO</th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">88.2</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">51.7</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">-</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;" rowspan="5"><span id="S5.T1.4.6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Diffusion</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.4.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">(baseline)</th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">79.7</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">35.6</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">10.4</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">CoT SFT</th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">78.8</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">34.8</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">14.5</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">UniGRPO</th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">73.4</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">36.0</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">-</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">diffu-GRPO</th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">79.8</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">39.2</td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">31.3</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">AGRPO (ours)</th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.10.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">87.3</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.10.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">40.8</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.10.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">40.0</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04019v1/pareto_frontier.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:411/245;" width="411" height="245"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>The inference compute/quality frontier for GSM8K across different configurations. Lines connect comparable points, i.e. same model and response length, showing the possible tradeoffs at inference time. Models trained with AGRPO consistently outperform baselines and additionally retain quality with fewer sampling steps.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report accuracies on test splits in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. For diffusion models, AGRPO achieves the highest accuracy across all three tasks, comfortably beating the baseline LLaDA-8B-Instruct model and other dLLM post-training methods.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            Chain-of-thought (CoT) SFT results are reported in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which notedly report slightly lower baseline numbers for GSM8K and MATH. Note also that UniGRPO starts training on top of MMaDA instead of LLaDA-8B-Instruct.</span></span></span> Although raw performance on GSM8K and MATH still lags behind autoregressive models, we observe similar performance gains from online RL (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 6\%</annotation></semantics></math> on GSM8K and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 5\%</annotation></semantics></math> on MATH) for both model types<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            GRPO is trained starting from the SFT stage, not the baseline DeepSeekMath-Base 7B model.</span></span></span>, suggesting that AGRPO is comparable to the original GRPO in terms of efficacy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A big advantage of using dLLMs is the ability to trade off compute and quality at inference time. We investigate how AGRPO affects such tradeoffs on GSM8K by fixing the response length at <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=384</annotation></semantics></math> and varying the number of sampling steps <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, not only does AGRPO expand the inference compute/quality frontier by consistently achieving higher performance, it matches the baseline with <em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">4x fewer sampling steps</em>, a remarkable speedup. We give sample responses for different values of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Sample responses ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our results firmly establish AGRPO as a viable way to transfer RL techniques to the dLLM setting, demonstrating that proper RLVR with unbiased policy gradients can instill robust reasoning skills regardless of model type. Additionally, these reasoning skills can complement dLLMs’ innate ability to trade off inference compute and quality, as seen in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work presents AGRPO, an online RL algorithm designed for dLLMs that computes unbiased policy gradient estimates via Monte Carlo sampling, making it the first such method that is both principled and tractable. After theoretically grounding AGRPO, we show how to effectively post-train dLLMs using our proposed algorithm, beating comparable methods across multiple tasks and redefining the inference compute/quality frontier. We hope future works can build on our methods, either theoretically or empirically, and further close the gap between dLLM and AR LLM post-training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Reproducibility statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present pseudocode and a discussion of implementation details in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.1 Deriving the AGRPO objective ‣ 4 Amortized GRPO ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Practical considerations ‣ 4 Amortized GRPO ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, respectively. The high-level setup for experiments is given in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiments ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. We refer readers to the appendix for a more detailed report of datasets, hyperparameters, implementation, and sample responses.</p>
</div>
</section>
