<p id="A1.I1.i6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\alpha=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\alpha=64</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A1.I1.i6.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{dropout}=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>dropout</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{dropout}=0.05</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AdamW:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1.i7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A1.I1.i7.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A1.I1.i7.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i7.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.99</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.1.2 </span>Datasets</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all tasks and train/test splits, we use open source datasets on HuggingFace. Reported GSM8K accuracies use GSM8K-Platinum <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vendrow et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, a cleaned version of the original GSM8K test split. MATH accuracy was reported from a model trained on NVIDIA’s OpenMathInstruct-2 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toshniwal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which consists of 14M augmented GSM8K and MATH problems (we only use the first 10,000). The Countdown dataset can be found at <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Jiayi-Pan/Countdown-Tasks-3to4" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets/Jiayi-Pan/Countdown-Tasks-3to4</a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use HuggingFace’s <span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">math-verify</span> library for parsing GSM8K and MATH answers.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Sample responses</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">See Table <a href="#A1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ A.2 Sample responses ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Sample responses (<math id="A1.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=384</annotation></semantics></math>) from a model trained with AGRPO for different values of <math id="A1.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> (# sampling steps) for a prompt from GSM8K-Platinum.</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A1.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">
<span id="A1.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:357.7pt;">
<span id="A1.T2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System:</span> You will be given a math problem.
Think through the problem step by step, enclosing your reasoning process in &lt;think&gt;tags.
Provide your final answer in &lt;answer&gt;tags, with only the number or expression enclosed in \boxed{}, as follows:
&lt;think&gt;…
&lt;/think&gt;&lt;answer&gt;\boxed{…}
&lt;/answer&gt;</span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A1.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">
<span id="A1.T2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:357.7pt;">
<span id="A1.T2.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T2.2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User:</span> 44 seniors need to receive awards. Each senior receives a picture frame that costs $20. Each picture frame needs to be etched with the logo for an additional 20% cost per frame. 2 of the seniors will also receive pins that are $5. 1/4 of the seniors are officers and they will need to receive cords that are $12 each. How much will be spent on the senior gifts?</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">
<span id="A1.T2.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:357.7pt;">
<span id="A1.T2.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T2.2.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assistant (m=192):</span>
&lt;think&gt;1. First, calculate the cost of each etched picture frame:
- The cost of one picture frame is $20.
- The additional cost for etching is 20% of $20, which is <math id="A1.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.20\times 20=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0.20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.20\times 20=4</annotation></semantics></math>.
- Therefore, the cost of each etched picture frame is <math id="A1.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20+4=24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20+4=24</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A1.T2.2.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">2. Calculate the total cost for the etched picture frames:
- There are 44 seniors, and each receives an etched picture frame costing $24.
- The total cost for the picture frames is <math id="A1.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="44\times 24=1056" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>44</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1056</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44\times 24=1056</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A1.T2.2.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_p">3. Calculate the total cost for the pins:
- 2 seniors will receive pins, and each pin costs $5.
- The total cost for the pins is <math id="A1.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 5=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 5=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A1.T2.2.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_p">4. Calculate the number of officers:
- 1/4 of the seniors are officers, so the number of officers is <math id="A1.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="frac{1}{4}\times 44=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>44</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">frac{1}{4}\times 44=11</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A1.T2.2.3.1.1.5" class="ltx_p">5. Calculate the total cost for the cords:
- Each cord costs $12.
- The total cost for the cords is <math id="A1.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11\times 12=132" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>132</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\times 12=132</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A1.T2.2.3.1.1.6" class="ltx_p">6. Calculate the total amount spent on the senior gifts:
- Add the total costs for the picture frames, pins, and cords.
- The total amount spent is <math id="A1.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1056+10+132=1198" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1056</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>132</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1198</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1056+10+132=1198</annotation></semantics></math>.
&lt;/thinkthink&gt;&lt;answer&gt;\boxed{1198}
&lt;/answer&gt;</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">
<span id="A1.T2.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:357.7pt;">
<span id="A1.T2.2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T2.2.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assistant (m=48):</span>
&lt;think&gt;1. First, calculate the cost of the picture frames:
- Each base picture frame picture costs $ $20</span>
<span id="A1.T2.2.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">- Etching costs an additional 20% of $ so0,
0.2000000000000000 20 = 4.
Therefore, the total cost per picture frame is 20 + 4 = 24.</span>
<span id="A1.T2.2.4.1.1.3" class="ltx_p">- Total cost for 44 seniors is 44 * 24 = 1056.</span>
<span id="A1.T2.2.4.1.1.4" class="ltx_p">2. Next, calculate the cost of pins:
- 2 seniors will receive pins, and each pin costs $5.
- Total cost for pins is 2 * 5 = $10.</span>
<span id="A1.T2.2.4.1.1.5" class="ltx_p">3. Finally, calculate the cost of cords:
- 1/4 of the seniors are officers, so there are 44 / 4 = 11 officers.
- Each cord costs $12.
- Total cost for cords is 11 * 12 = $132.</span>
<span id="A1.T2.2.4.1.1.6" class="ltx_p">4. Add, up all the costs:
- Total cost = picture frames is $1056 + pins is $10 + cords is $132.
- Total cost = 1056 + 10 + 132 = $1198.
&lt;/think&gt;&lt;answer&gt;\boxed{1198}
&lt;/answer&gt;</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Limitations</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since AGRPO involves multiple forward passes per gradient step, it is inherently quite compute-hungry, and training can be quite slow. Furthermore, there are many potential ways to estimate the GRPO objective with Monte Carlo sampling; sampling along the time axis is a natural way, but there could other ways, e.g. importance sampling, that optimally reduce variance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, we observe that some models trained with AGRPO with high reward can exhibit signs of entropy collapse, namely reduced token diversity and formulaic, “template”-y answers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Further practical considerations</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">float64 Gumbel-based categorical sampling</span> dLLMs typically use the Gumbel-max trick to sample from output logits. However, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2025)</a></cite> point out that naively using <span id="A1.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">float32</span> causes an inconsistency between theoretical and actual behavior due to numerical instability. We follow their recommendation of using <span id="A1.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">float64</span> for the sampling stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Handling EOS tokens</span> Since dLLMs generate with a fixed number of sampling steps, in the later stages, the model can spend many “garbage” steps producing <span id="A1.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EOS</span> tokens at the end of a sequence (while other sequences in the batch are still generating useful tokens). Gradient updates on these steps don’t provide meaningful information to the model, so we set the max timestep in our low-discrepancy sampler (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Low-discrepancy sampling ‣ 4 Amortized GRPO ‣ Principled and Tractable RL for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) to be the last timestep a non-<span id="A1.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EOS</span> token was generated.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Remarks on dLLM inference</h3>

<section id="A1.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.5.1 </span>Instruct-tuned models</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">dLLMs such as LLaDA-8B-Instruct, which have undergone supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on instruction-following traces, tend to place higher probabilities on <span id="A1.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EOS</span> tokens. When combined with low-confidence unmasking, this leads to an unnaturally high proportion of <span id="A1.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EOS</span> tokens in later positions and terse, stilted responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, to generate text with standard left-to-right prompting, we divide the response into smaller blocks, unmask tokens within the leftmost block, and continue to the next block once all tokens in the current block have been unmasked. This is known as <em id="A1.SS5.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semi-autoregressive</em> sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In our experiments, we use semi-autoregressive sampling with a block size of 24 or 32.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.5.2 </span>Bidirectional prompting</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we work with traditional left-to-right prompting, which is the native format for the reasoning datasets we use. This leaves a big dLLM advantage on the table — namely their ability to generate text from arbitrary context.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Future works could consider designing reasoning tasks that involve using context from both the left and right; for example, giving the model a problem and the numerical answer, and forcing it to deduce the intermediate steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.5.3 </span>KV caching</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since dLLMs use full self-attention instead of causal self-attention, embeddings for the same token position can change depending on the sampling step. This fundamentally prevents dLLMs from using the same KV caching mechanism as AR LLMs. As a result, generating same-quality text with dLLMs is significantly slower than same-scale AR models, which is especially painful for online RL. However, there has been some recent interest in KV caching alternatives for dLLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since dLLMs already have the ability to unmask multiple tokens at once, we believe a successful implementation of KV caching will be the key to realizing dLLMs’ potential as a faster, more flexible alternative to AR models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
